### ✅ **Safe `deepEqual` for JSON-like structures**

```ts
function deepEqual(a: any, b: any): boolean {
  // Strict equality handles primitives, null, undefined
  if (a === b) return true;

  // Different types can’t be equal
  if (typeof a !== typeof b) return false;

  // Handle null safely (typeof null === 'object'!)
  if (a === null || b === null) return false;

  // Arrays
  if (Array.isArray(a)) {
    if (!Array.isArray(b)) return false;
    if (a.length !== b.length) return false;
    for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
      if (!deepEqual(a[i], b[i])) return false;
    }
    return true;
  }

  // Objects (not arrays)
  if (typeof a === 'object') {
    if (Array.isArray(b)) return false; // b is array, a is not => not equal

    const aKeys = Object.keys(a);
    const bKeys = Object.keys(b);

    if (aKeys.length !== bKeys.length) return false;

    for (const key of aKeys) {
      if (!b.hasOwnProperty(key)) return false;
      if (!deepEqual(a[key], b[key])) return false;
    }

    return true;
  }

  // For functions, symbols, other weird types, return false
  return false;
}
```

---

### 📌 **Why this is clearer**

✅ *Handles `null`* explicitly — no more `typeof null` confusion.  
✅ *Arrays and plain objects* are handled in distinct blocks.  
✅ *Mixed types* (array vs. object) fail immediately.  
✅ *No redundant checks* — it’s obvious what’s happening at each step.  
✅ *Strict equality first* catches all primitives and identical references.

---

### 🗝️ **Example tests**

```ts
console.log(deepEqual({ a: 1 }, { a: 1 })); // true
console.log(deepEqual({ a: 1 }, { a: 2 })); // false
console.log(deepEqual([1, 2], [1, 2])); // true
console.log(deepEqual([1, 2], [2, 1])); // false
console.log(deepEqual({ a: [1, 2] }, { a: [1, 2] })); // true
console.log(deepEqual({ a: [1, 2] }, { a: [2, 1] })); // false
```

